  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a dianionic dicarboxylic acid at pH 7.4 (logD -3.24, neutral fraction 0, TPSA 126.8) with no basic center; the closest measured quinolone acid, norfloxacin, has low intrinsic PAMPA permeability (0.7 × 10^-6 cm/s) and only moderate aqueous solubility at pH 7 (0.31–0.45 mg/mL), indicating a major absorption barrier that should be worse for the more polar query.",
      "card_ids": ["C156","C133","C159"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Oxolinic acid, a less polar monoacid quinolone, has only 13.8 µg/mL solubility at pH 7, illustrating poor dissolution for quinolone acids; the query's two carboxylates increase aqueous solubility but also make it fully ionized and less membrane-permeable.",
      "card_ids": ["C140"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Direct low oral bioavailability outcomes for norfloxacin in sheep (4.04%) and donkeys (9.6%), plus limited ruminant bioavailability, are the closest affirmative threshold-relevant F data and support F < 20% for the more polar query.",
      "card_ids": ["C28","C40","C37"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Human norfloxacin F values cluster around 35–45% (range 22–70%), showing that even a single-carboxyl quinolone acid is incompletely absorbed; the dianionic query is expected to be lower.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C08","C09","C23","C33","C42","C20"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 quinolone efflux records (enoxacin ER 1.8 considered P-gp substrate; ofloxacin ER 1.7; levofloxacin in vivo low permeability attributed to P-gp) provide weak mechanistic support for presystemic loss, but transferability is limited because the query lacks the basic piperazine and is dianionic.",
      "card_ids": ["C186","C185","C162","C198"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records are mostly low-transferability: enrofloxacin shows moderate HLM intrinsic clearance (16.09 µL/min/mg, t1/2 43.1 min), weakly supporting possible hepatic first-pass loss, while levofloxacin UGT Km values are high (2.6–6.0 mM), suggesting low glucuronidation affinity; neither is direct query evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C236","C235","C239","C229"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "High-absorption records for basic zwitterionic quinolones such as ofloxacin, levofloxacin, pefloxacin, enoxacin, and fleroxacin do not transfer to the dianionic query because they rely on a basic center and zwitterionic character that the query lacks.",
      "card_ids": ["C123","C145","C132","C146","C121","C139","C138","C114"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C236","C235","C239","C229"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No direct permeability, solubility, efflux, or hepatic clearance measurement exists for the query itself.",
    "L6 hepatic-clearance records are from other molecules (enrofloxacin, levofloxacin, DMXAA, repaglinide, etc.) with limited structural transfer to the dianionic query.",
    "Norfloxacin low-F data are from non-human species (sheep, donkeys) and may not translate exactly to human F.",
    "The physicochemical prior is estimated rather than measured.",
    "No information on active transport or prodrug behavior is available."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Keep low at moderate confidence. L6 hepatic-clearance records are mostly low-transferability (enrofloxacin moderate HLM CLint 16.09 µL/min/mg; levofloxacin UGT Km high) and do not change the prediction; the dianionic dicarboxylic acid query remains predicted below 20% based on poor permeability and the closest direct low-F quinolone outcomes."
}

